# crawl-without-seed.tflw — CHECK-ONLY FIXTURE (M137c): a `crawl` that declares no `seed`, so its
# surface is empty before the run starts — demonstrates `tflw check`'s TF068 (checker, tflw M137c /
# D443, the pentest arc's Tier 4).
#
# A crawl does not name its routes, it **discovers** them, from the `seed` clauses in its header. With
# none it issues no request at all — so every assertion in its body judges nothing and could not have
# failed whatever the application did. That is D285's no-power-to-fail rule arriving on Tier 4's new
# construct, and it earns its own code because the repair is specific: give the crawl something to
# crawl.
#
# **The span is the header, not the first assertion.** The bodies below are correct. Sending a reader
# to a line that is right is worse than saying nothing, so the diagnostic underlines `crawl "…"`.
#
# **The same code has a runtime door, and the split is the whole design.** Zero `seed` clauses is a
# fact about the *file*, decidable here. An OpenAPI document that answers 404, a run whose own tests
# captured no traffic, and an `exclude` list that happens to cover every route discovered are facts
# about the *run* — the interpreter refuses those, **reusing** this code rather than minting a second
# one, because the repair reads the same from either side. Only the static half is demonstrable in a
# check-only fixture; the runtime half needs a live server and belongs to the acceptance corpus.
#
# **`exclude` is not a seed**, which is the second crawl below. It subtracts from a surface and cannot
# supply one — and a crawl carrying only an `exclude` is the shape most likely to look seeded at a
# glance, which is exactly why it is worth a case of its own.
#
# **What must NOT fire here, and every one of them is a recorded decision rather than a gap:**
#   * `TF039` (assert before any request) — a crawl body has no preceding request **by construction**,
#     because the crawl is what issues them. `checkResponseScopes` is deliberately not wired to crawl
#     bodies (tflw D464); wiring it would have put TF039 on every correct crawl that will ever be
#     written, which is a checker refusing the only shape the feature has.
#   * `TF060` (a security assertion with no `authorized target`) — this **is** wired to crawl bodies
#     via `forEachExpect`, and stays silent only because this project's own config declares a target.
#     If the root config ever loses it, this file goes red, and that is correct: a crawl is the most
#     traffic-originating construct in the language and must not be the one that skips a D21 gate.
#   * `TF067` (an input-handling assertion with nothing to mutate) — a rule about an `api` step's own
#     literal shape, and a crawl body has no `api` step to read. `TF070` is what refuses one there.
#
# Run explicitly (dot-directory, invalid syntax — see bad-keyword.tflw's header for why):
#   npx tflw check tests/.checkonly/crawl-without-seed.tflw

crawl "a surface nobody described"
  expect response has no critical security violations

crawl "an exclude cannot supply a surface"
  exclude "/internal/**"
  expect response has no critical security violations

# --- the negative controls: one seed clause each, and TF068 goes quiet ---------------------------
#
# Without these, a future widening of the rule — refusing `seed traffic` for having no document to
# read, say — would pass unnoticed, because the firing half above would keep firing either way. A
# fixture that only demonstrates the loud half cannot tell a rule from an over-reach.

crawl "the documented surface"
  seed openapi "/openapi.json"
  expect response has no critical security violations

crawl "the surface this run's own tests already touched" as shopper, peer
  # Both seeds and an `exclude` together, which is the shape a real suite writes. Two principals
  # rather than one because Tier 2's oracle is differential: `authorization violations` compares what
  # one identity may reach against what another may, so a single-principal crawl has nothing to
  # subtract. That is also why the two crawls above assert `security` only — they declare no `as`.
  seed openapi "/openapi.json"
  seed traffic
  exclude "/internal/**"
  expect response has no critical security violations
  expect response has no critical authorization violations
  # `check` is the soft form of the same matcher, so it is accepted here for the same reason
  # `expect` is — the crawl-body rule turns on the *matcher*, not on whether the step is fatal.
  check response has no input handling violations
